      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-2{t}^{2}+2t+12,\\qquad y'(t)=3{t}^{2}-3t-6.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-3t-6 & =0 \\\\ 3(t-2)(t+1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-1) & =8\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(2) & =8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-1$ and $t=2$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/id": "d4f121dc-20d5-5eda-9d00-aee680d617e5"
    },
